package events

// Register installs the application's event listeners on d. It is the stable,
// user-editable seam for events: `weld add events` writes this file once and
// never regenerates it, so the listeners you add here survive every later
// `weld add`.
//
// The default implementation registers no listeners, which is why installing the
// events capability dispatches nothing by itself. Add one with On, keyed on the
// event's concrete Go type:
//
//	func Register(d *Dispatcher) error {
//		On(d, func(ctx context.Context, event ItemCreated) error {
//			return mailer.SendWelcome(ctx, event.ID)
//		})
//		return nil
//	}
//
// Register runs once, when the Loom graph constructs the dispatcher, so a
// listener that needs configuration can fail startup loudly here rather than at
// the first publish.
//
// Two rules worth knowing before you add a listener:
//
//   - Delivery is synchronous. The listener runs in the caller's goroutine, so
//     keep it fast; a slow listener slows the request that published the event.
//     A queue capability is the answer for slow work, not a bare goroutine.
//   - An event published inside a database transaction is delivered before the
//     transaction commits, so publish after the commit when a listener must not
//     observe a rolled-back change.
func Register(d *Dispatcher) error {
	return nil
}
